    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, the Zurich Zebras lean heavily on **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13), who headlines our lineup as the premier play. Facing Pallante, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from an optimal leadoff spot and an aggressive matchup against a pitcher susceptible to his elite sprint speed, compounded by a \"Slow SP Delivery\" factor that boosts his stolen base probability. Similarly, **Yordan Alvarez** (73.90) and **Junior Caminero** (73.43) provide high-floor production; **Yordan Alvarez** remains a lock given his elite xwOBA, while **Junior Caminero** draws a favorable matchup against Ure\u00f1a, whose 4.58 xERA suggests a high likelihood of scoring and RBI opportunities in the middle of our order.\n\nWe have strategically leveraged platoon edges to maximize our 5x5 categories, most notably with **Kazuma Okamoto** (67.98), who benefits significantly from a favorable park factor and a platoon advantage against Rogers. While **Christian Yelich** (65.69) is also a strong start, the decision to leave **Carson Benge** (48.18) on the bench was the most difficult call of the morning. While **Carson Benge** profiles well as a leadoff hitter, his lower raw efficiency against Meyer ultimately pushed him behind our high-upside starters. We continue to monitor the status of **Lars Nootbaar**, who remains unavailable due to his IL designation, further thinning our options for utility rotation.\n\nRisks remain present, particularly regarding the matchup against Wrobleski, which slightly suppresses the scores for **Trea Turner** (59.12) and **Kyle Schwarber** (57.48). Despite the SP skill disadvantage for **Trea Turner**, his projected stolen base volume remains vital to our strategy in that scarcest of categories. Meanwhile, **Caleb Durbin** (52.44) is locked in as a defensive and speed-focused MI play, though his upside is checked by a bottom-of-the-order assignment against an efficient catcher. We are proceeding with this lineup to maintain our competitive edge in stolen bases while ensuring we do not exhaust our game cap allotment on marginal outcomes.",
